y}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:68cd4bd1bb7f3d5224747e115d38a45dbb190726881cea37366148bb7d85ced8", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1b80ed4819a4552f5ba0191e63039c0d59bc4e5ef63bc91486a262c63188843a", "source_text": "As <MATH_0001> agrees for modules and abelian sheaves by\nLemma <REF_0001>\nit suffices to prove this when <MATH_0002> is an abelian sheaf.\nFor <MATH_0003> the abelian sheaf <MATH_0004>\nis a projective object of <MATH_0005> since\n<MATH_0006>\nand taking sections is an exact functor as the topology is chaotic.\nEvery abelian sheaf is a quotient of a direct sum of <MATH_0007>\nby Modules on Sites, Lemma <REF_0002>.\nThus we can compute <MATH_0008> by choosing a resolution\n<MATH_0009>\nwhose terms are direct sums of sheaves of the form above and taking\n<MATH_0010>. Consider the\ndouble complex\n<MATH_0011>.\nThe map <MATH_0012> gives a map of complexes\n<MATH_0013>.\nSince <MATH_0014> is computed by taking the colimit over\n<MATH_0015> (Lemma <REF_0003>)\nwe see that the two compositions\n<MATH_0016>\nare equal. Thus we obtain a canonical map of complexes\n<MATH_0017>\nTo prove the lemma it suffices to show that the complexes\n<MATH_0018>\nare exact, see Homology, Lemma\n<REF_0004>.\nSince the sheaves <MATH_0019> are direct sums of the sheaves\n<MATH_0020> we reduce to <MATH_0021>.\nThe complex <MATH_0022>\nis the complex of abelian groups associated to the free\n<MATH_0023>-module on the simplicial set\n<MATH_0024> which we assumed to be homotopy\nequivalent to a singleton. We conclude that\n<MATH_0025>\nis a homotopy equivalence of abelian groups hence a quasi-isomorphism\n(Simplicial, Remark <REF_0005> and\nLemma <REF_0006>). This finishes the proof\nsince <MATH_0026>\nas was shown in the proof of\nLemma <REF_0007>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b6efe2